  "query_preview": "/Through Monday/...\nIssued at 1153 AM MDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nCloud cover made for a mild morning today, and has also slowed the\ndiurnal warming somewhat. ACARS soundings show abundant moisture\nstill in place (PW ~1\" currently), and this should only increase\nas the day progresses. Instability, which is a notch lower than\nyesterday, will likely be maximized within the zone of southerly\nflow south and east of the DCVZ. That said, the axis of better \nshear is not particularly coincident, being shifted farther north\nover the I-76 corridor where flow aloft will be stronger given \nthe approaching trough and jet. This makes it difficult to \npinpoint one specific area of greater coverage for afternoon \nshowers and thunderstorms. In any case, scattered showers look to\ndevelop mid-afternoon over the urban corridor with potential for isolated\nand mostly weak thunderstorms. The environment would support some\nstrengthening into the eastern plains where a few storms could be\nstrong to marginally severe later this afternoon and evening. \nTemperatures will trend down for most areas given increased cloud \ncover and upslope flow to the north of the DCVZ, with the warmest \ntemperatures focused over our southeast plains.\n\nThe upper-level trough will traverse the northern CONUS Tuesday,\npushing a weak cold front through the plains and lowering\ntemperatures 3-8 degrees for most areas. This will also lead to\nmore stable conditions and erosion of moisture across the northern\nhalf of the forecast area, reducing precipitation chances\nconsiderably. Areas roughly along and south of I-70, where better\ninstability, shear and PW will overlap, will remain favored for \nafternoon convection and some locally heavy rain.\n\nDrier westerly flow will settle into the region Wednesday as a\nzonal pattern takes hold, potentially making Wednesday the coolest\nday of the week with highs in the low/mid 80's (only Sunday could\nchallenge that). Most will stay dry, though a chance of an\nafternoon shower or thunderstorm will linger for Elbert and\nLincoln Counties. Those chances evaporate come Thursday as\nmoisture aloft decreases further. Temperatures look to rebound \nback to above-normal values. Expect little change in those \nconditions into Friday.\n\nForecast confidence lowers over the course of the weekend as models\nbegin to diverge in their treatment of an amplifying trough over\nthe PacNW. Most hint a subtle shortwave embedded within zonal flow\non Saturday and an associated weak cold front bringing initially\ncooler temperatures and a return of precipitation chances. The\nmore significant cooldown however will likely wait until either\nSunday or Monday, depending on the timing of the stronger front \nas the deeper trough approaches and lifts through the Northern \nRockies. Flow aloft will shift back to the southwest starting\nSunday with a strengthening jet in the mix as well. As such, we \nshould see a better window for a return to unsettled weather and \npotentially some stronger thunderstorms and locally heavy rain,\nbut of course all of those details remain quite fuzzy.",
